1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,190
You know the most famous quote about Pearl

2
00:00:02,190 --> 00:00:05,380
Harbor. Nobody can find it before 1970.

3
00:00:05,958 --> 00:00:08,058
“I fear all we have done is to awaken a

4
00:00:08,058 --> 00:00:10,558
sleeping giant and fill him with a terrible

5
00:00:10,558 --> 00:00:11,188
resolve.”

6
00:00:11,889 --> 00:00:14,399
It's credited to Admiral Isoroku Yamamoto,

7
00:00:14,989 --> 00:00:16,419
the man who planned the attack.

8
00:00:16,929 --> 00:00:18,389
It's on posters, in documentaries,

9
00:00:18,389 --> 00:00:19,479
in classrooms.

10
00:00:20,236 --> 00:00:22,206
But there is no evidence he ever said it.

11
00:00:22,576 --> 00:00:25,016
The line comes from a Hollywood movie: Tora!

12
00:00:25,016 --> 00:00:27,746
Tora! Tora!, released in 1970.

13
00:00:28,407 --> 00:00:30,527
The real Yamamoto had studied at Harvard

14
00:00:30,527 --> 00:00:33,637
and served as a naval attaché in Washington.

15
00:00:34,287 --> 00:00:36,187
He had seen what American factories could

16
00:00:36,187 --> 00:00:36,637
build.

17
00:00:37,034 --> 00:00:38,924
Before the war, he wrote a private letter.

18
00:00:39,474 --> 00:00:40,364
To win, he said,

19
00:00:40,554 --> 00:00:42,474
Japan would have to march into Washington

20
00:00:42,474 --> 00:00:44,684
and dictate peace in the White House.

21
00:00:45,032 --> 00:00:47,432
Then he asked if Japan's politicians were

22
00:00:47,432 --> 00:00:48,802
ready for that kind of sacrifice.

23
00:00:49,668 --> 00:00:51,698
The letter spread without that last line.

24
00:00:52,168 --> 00:00:53,898
In America, it read like a boast.

25
00:00:54,308 --> 00:00:55,018
It was a warning.

26
00:00:55,665 --> 00:00:57,595
The words he never said made him a prophet.

27
00:00:58,185 --> 00:00:59,855
The words he did write made him a villain.

28
00:01:00,305 --> 00:01:01,615
Which version did you learn?

